    "summary": "Historic debut of Ripple, Truckin' & stellar acoustic set",
    "blurb": "The show delivers the first live renditions of Ripple and Truckin', backed by a superb acoustic opening. Jerry's guitar and vocals shine throughout, and the electric set packs historic jams and tight transitions.",
    "review": "August 18, 1970 at Fillmore West stands out as a landmark in Dead history – it’s the night we first heard Ripple and Truckin' live, and the band delivers those songs with fresh excitement. The acoustic set is described as “superb” and “very well‑executed,” showcasing Jerry’s warm tone on Ripple and his vocal clarity on New Speedway Boogie. The electric half keeps the momentum high, featuring a seamless Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One sequence, a joyous Dancin' In The Street, and powerful renditions of Attics Of My Life and It's A Man's World. Reviewers consistently note the historic significance and the band’s locked‑in playing, calling the performance “a gift from the gods.” While one tape suffers from speed issues, the matrix mix (gd1970-08-18.141071.aud.pitch-adjusted.flac1644) offers crystal‑clear sound that captures both the intimacy of the acoustic numbers and the expansive energy of the electric set, making it the definitive listening experience.",
